    {
      "id": "far-jl-ledger-c1",
      "heading": "What the ledger is and how it is laid out",
      "explanation_md": "The **ledger** is the **principal book of the accounting system**. It holds the different accounts, and the transactions that relate to each account are recorded in that account.\n\nThe format has a **Dr.** side on the left and a **Cr.** side on the right. Each side has the same columns: **Date**, **Particulars**, **J.F.** and **Amount (₹)**.\n\nPosting means taking each journal entry and writing the debit amount on the debit side of one account and the credit amount on the credit side of the other. The journal uses the L.F. column; the ledger uses J.F.",
      "example_ids": [],
      "key_terms": [
        {
          "term": "Ledger",
          "meaning": "The principal book of the accounting system, containing the different accounts where transactions relating to each account are recorded.",
          "source_ref": "far--4-co3-journal-ledger-tb#9"
        }
      ],
      "exam_line": "The ledger is the principal book of accounts. Format: Dr. side and Cr. side, each with Date, Particulars, J.F. and Amount.",
      "clarification": null,
