    {
      "id": "far-jl-ledger-c2",
      "heading": "Cash T-account: classify, post and find the balance",
      "explanation_md": "The question gives three journal entries and asks for three things: **classify each account and say its normal balance**, **post to the Cash account in T-account format** (opening balance 10,000 on March 1), and **find the closing cash balance**.\n\nThe entries are:\n\n- March 2: **Cash A/c Dr, To Paid-in Capital** 3,00,000 (capital issued).\n- March 31: **Salary Expense A/c Dr, To Salary Payable A/c** 50,000 (accrued wages owed).\n- April 1: **Supplies A/c Dr, To Cash A/c** 90,000 (cash paid for supplies).\n\n**Normal balances:** Cash is an asset, so debit. Supplies is an asset, so debit. Salary Expense is an expense, so debit. Paid-in Capital is on the credit side. Salary Payable is a payable, so credit.\n\n**Cash T-account:** Debit side: opening balance 10,000 and March 2 capital 3,00,000. Credit side: April 1 supplies 90,000. The salary entry does not touch Cash.\n\n**Balance:** 10,000 + 3,00,000 - 90,000 = **₹2,20,000 debit**.",
      "example_ids": [
        "far-ex-ex-cash-tacct"
      ],
      "key_terms": [],
      "exam_line": "Cash closing balance: 10,000 + 3,00,000 - 90,000 = 2,20,000 (debit). The salary accrual does not touch Cash.",
